      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Lexington’s name honors either Lord Lexington, an English peer, or the English town of Laxton in Nottinghamshire.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lexington%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Every Patriots’ Day, Lexington reenactors stage a dramatic Battle of Lexington on the historic Green.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lexington%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Minute Man National Historical Park, encompassing Lexington’s Battle Green, preserves the site of America’s first armed conflict.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lexington%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The former Boston and Maine train station, now the Lexington Historical Society headquarters, sits beside the town’s historic depot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
